- How would you extend to cycles and determine worst-case iterations?
- How would you optimize for sparse graphs and pruning impossible combinations?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests cross-time propagation in three-valued logic, simulates realistic policy engine scenarios, and validates backtracking capabilities with multi-step constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic
- Cross-temporal constraint satisfaction
- Backtracking with propagation and pruning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple two-period CSP checker with Kleene semantics
function kleeneNot(x) { if(x==='T') return 'F'; if(x==='F') return 'T'; return 'U'; }
function kleeneOr(a,b) { if(a==='T'||b==='T') return 'T'; if(a==='U'||b==='U') return 'U'; return 'F'; }
function kleeneAnd(a,b) { if(a==='F'||b==='F') return 'F'; if(a==='U'||b==='U') return 'U'; return 'T'; }
function kleeneImplies(a,b) { return kleeneOr(kleeneNot(a), b); }

// Check constraints across both periods
function validateConstraints(t0, t1) {
  const withinT0 = [
    kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t0.C1, t0.C2), t0.C3),
    kleeneOr(kleeneNot(t0.C5), t0.C4),
    kleeneImplies(kleeneAnd(t0.C2, kleeneNot(t0.C3)), kleeneOr(t0.C1, t0.C5))
  ];
  const withinT1 = [
    kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t1.C1, t1.C2), t1.C3),
    kleeneOr(kleeneNot(t1.C5), t1.C4),
    kleeneImplies(kleeneAnd(t1.C2, kleeneNot(t1.C3)), kleeneOr(t1.C1, t1.C5))
  ];
  const cross = kleeneImplies(kleeneOr(t0.C1, t1.C4), t0.C2);

  return [...withinT0, ...withinT1, cross].every(v => v !== 'F');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about combined implications and negation, and to design a small exhaustive search with pruning.

## Key Concepts

- Implication and negation in boolean constraints
- Exhaustive search with pruning
- Small-scope backtracking for correctness checks

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve() {
  for (let A = 0; A <= 1; A++) {
    for (let B = 0; B <= 1; B++) {
      for (let C = 0; C <= 1; C++) {
        const ok1 = (!A) || B;     // A -> B
        const ok2 = (!B) || (!C);  // B -> ¬C
        const ok3 = (A || B || C); // at least one true
        if (ok1 && ok2 && ok3) return {A,B,C};
      }
    }
  }
  return null;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-step reasoning in a tri-state logic with temporal separation. Candidates must show how to craft a concrete non-F assignment and outline a propagation strategy across two time steps.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics for ∧, ∨, ¬, and → via ¬X ∨ Y
- Temporal layering: constraints reference values at t and t+1
- Unit/propagation approach under three-valued logic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Kleene 3-valued helper and constraint evaluator
const order = { F: 0, U: 1, T: 2 };
function not(v){ return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function min(a,b){ return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b; }
function max(a,b){ return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b; }
function implies(a,b){ return max(not(a), b); }
function vOr(a,b){ return max(a,b); }
function vAnd(a,b){ return min(a,b); }

function evalConstraints(s){ // s has A0,B0,C0,D0,A1,B1,C1,D1
  const c1 = implies(vAnd(s.A0,s.B0), s.C0);
  const c2 = implies(vOr(s.B0, s.D0), s.A0);
  const c3 = implies(not(s.D1), vOr(s.A1, s.C0));
  return [c1,c2,c3];
}

const state = {A0:'T',B0:'T',C0:'T',D0:'F',A1:'F',B1:'F',C1:'F',D1:'U'};
console.log(evalConstraints(state));
```

